  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ef/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_9.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man rushes through town in such a hurry, he's not paying attention to anything else (including an active crime scene) until he arrives home, and we learn that he was out filling Aunt May's prescription.&nbsp; Apparently her illness is serious.&nbsp; Meanwhile, a new character named Electro suits up and gives himself a full charge before going out and robbing an armored car filled with gold.\n\nThe next morning, the visiting doctor tells Peter that his Aunt May is worse and needs to go to the hospital.&nbsp; He ignores everyone in school before rushing to the hospital, where the doctor is preparing to operate, and he finds Betty Brant sitting with his aunt.&nbsp; He greets them before heading back out as Spider-Man -- he has to raise money for the operation, and the only way to do that is to take pictures of Spider-Man and sell them to the Bugle, even while Betty ponders why it always seems Peter \"carries a deep secret within him -- one which no one can ever share!\"\n\nRain spoils Peter's plan (his camera isn't good enough to take photos in such conditions), and he returns home, worried about money, preventing his spider suit from shrinking, Aunt May, <b><i>and</i></b> his upcoming midterm exams.\n\nThe next day features J. Jonah Jameson visiting his bank when Electro barges in!&nbsp; Electro <i>recognizes</i> Jameson, which the latter thinks is perhaps a clue that they're acquaintances.&nbsp; Electro robs the bank and then is able to scale the wall using electricity to lock onto its iron beams.&nbsp; Jameson jumps to the conclusion that Electro is actually Spider-Man, especially since Spider-Man <i>himself</i> swings by a moment later!&nbsp; The next edition of the Daily Bugle headlines with ELECTRO IS REALLY SPIDER-MAN!\n\nPeter is irritated about this, but he's preoccupied at the hospital, <i>especially</i> when he learns that the specialist they've brought in to operate on May charges $1,000!&nbsp; He tries to get Jameson to lend him the money, but he refuses (thinking Peter only wants the money to buy a \"hot rod\").&nbsp; Spider-Man then scours the city, hoping to find Electro and collect the reward money for his capture.&nbsp; After some false alarms, he eventually runs across the new villain.&nbsp; Spider-Man tries to grab him but is shocked by Electro, and he crumples.&nbsp; Electro thinks he's dead (and even thinks, \"I didn't mean to do that\") and leaves.\n\nWhen Peter wakes up, he decides to take pictures of himself superimposed over Electro so that Jameson will think he snapped Electro changing into Spider-Man.&nbsp; He hates selling these photos to Jameson under false pretenses, but he desperately needs the money!&nbsp; Jameson prints the photos on the front page as proof that Spider-Man is Electro, which makes the latter laugh and think about his former self, an electrician named Max Dillon.&nbsp; But one day, while he was up on the pole, a million-to-one electric bolt gave him the power to control electricity, which he practiced with before adopting the moniker Electro.\n\nNow he heads to the prison to free some inmates who can help fend off the police during his crime spree.&nbsp; But Aunt May calls for Peter just before her operation, and Peter promises to wait, with Betty showing up to keep him company.&nbsp; Meanwhile, Jameson is thrilled that Spider-Man isn't showing up at the prison to stop Electro because it proves that Electro <i>is</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; Electro is able to convince some inmates to wait and follow his lead.\n\nAt the hospital, the operation is a success, and Peter greets his recovering aunt before heading out.&nbsp; Betty urges him to stay and not go after the dangerous photos, sensing that Peter is starting to enjoy the danger \"just like someone <i>else</i> I once knew.\"&nbsp; But Peter heads out, and soon Spider-Man stops by a store to pick up a few supplies before heading out to face Electro (leaving money behind in payment).&nbsp; When Spider-Man arrives to face Electro, Jameson realizes he's been tricked and buries his head.&nbsp; He'll be a laughing stock, \"and all because of Peter Parker!\"\n\nParker pauses long enough to put on rubber gloves and galoshes to protect himself from Electro's body, and he proceeds to hurl metal objects at Electro, which deflect the oncoming bolts, but Electro knows he'll run out of things to throw soon.&nbsp; Electro swings electrified metal wires at him, but Spider-Man grabs a water main hose and douses Electro with it, short-circuiting him!&nbsp; After unmasking him and realizing he has no idea who Electro is, he heads toward the Bugle to give Jameson the new photos.\n\nJameson is livid and demands that \"blanketty-blank #$%#*\" be shown in, threatening to sue!&nbsp; But Peter stops him with genuine action shots of Spider-Man fighting Electro!&nbsp; Jameson changes his tune (of course) and offers to take these pictures in exchange for the fake one, to which Peter agrees.&nbsp; Jameson knows he's robbing him and that he'll make a fortune with these photos, but Parker deserves it because he's a fool!\n\nPeter and Betty have a brief spat about how Betty was hurt before, but the two eventually walk off together.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Peter Parker's life explodes with problems, and the reader can't look away.\nEven Electro can't compete with the human drama packed within these pages.",
  "subtitle": "More Voltage in Peter's Life Than in Electro",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | SplashPage | width=11%] First off, [SPAN: severity=success; text=kudos on that splash page, Steve Ditko!]&nbsp; What a <i>fantastic</i> representation of the world and characters surrounding both Peter Parker and Spider-Man, with Electro perched above it all, likely serving as catalyst for most, if not <i>all</i> of it.&nbsp; The caption below indicates that the story will feature both [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"human interest and drama.\"]&nbsp; And it is the former that I believe sets the magazine apart from its contemporaries.\n\nConsider all the things that <i>just the first six pages</i> spotlight:\n[HANG] * Aunt May's illness that requires her to go to the hospital\n[HANG] * Flash Thompson's momentary spark of decency toward Peter Parker\n[HANG] * Betty Brant's concern about the \"deep secret\" Peter seems to carry within\n[HANG] * The need to raise money to pay for Aunt May's operation\n[HANG] * Mundane matters such as washing his Spider-Man suit to avoid shrinkage\n[HANG] * Upcoming midterm exams\n\n[ASSET: left | LikeAMother | width=10%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=It's an <i>astonishing </i>array of issues that any one person has to deal with, and a <i>teenager</i> at that!]\n[BR]\n[BR] And though the first six pages <i>do</i> also contain the Electro introduction, they're really all about Peter's life, and its multitude of problems (or \"human interest\" as Stan puts in the splash page).\n[BR]\n[BR] And it's why the splash page's foreshadowed image is so spot-on.\n\nIt's clear enough in the opening pages that [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>nothing</i> is more important than Peter's aunt] -- not an active crime scene, nor Flash Thompson attempting to treat him like a human being for the first time.&nbsp; Getting through the school day and making a beeline toward the hospital is... well... <b><i>everything</i></b>.&nbsp; The fear of losing the one person left alive who has shown him love is paramount.\n\nRemember that we saw a flicker of this sentiment as far back as <i>Amazing Fantasy </i>15 ([LINK:1962:AMAZFANT_15_asm0|AMAZFANT #15|2026-04-01]), where on its page 8, Peter thinks to himself (about his aunt and uncle), [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"I'll see to it that <i>they're</i> always happy, but the rest of the world can go hang for all I care!\"]\n\nThese opening pages show him doing <i>exactly</i> that:&nbsp; using his Spider-Man powers to ensure Aunt May gets the medicine she needs and pretty much ignoring the rest of the world.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=It's a powerful thru line that threads its way across the issues we've seen since.]\n[BR] And it keeps Peter's portrayal consistent right from the very beginning.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ElectroIsSpiderMan | width=10%] As if Peter Parker's troubles weren't enough, Jameson's hasty conclusion in his paper's headline that [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=ELECTRO IS REALLY SPIDER-MAN] rankles Peter.\n[BR]\n[BR] Yet the poor boy has no time to dwell on the accusation, as page 8 shows him having to come up with a whopping sum of [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"one thousand dollars\"] to pay for the specialist brought in to perform Aunt May's operation.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: We may smirk <i>today</i> at what seems like a paltry sum, but in those days, that represented a <i>couple of months' pay</i> even for many working adults!&nbsp; In other words, it was no laughing matter!]\n\nPeter's desperation leads to his first encounter with Electro, and not realizing that to touch him is to receive an electric jolt, he crumples -- seemingly killed.&nbsp; Even Electro appears momentarily taken aback.\n[ASSET: center | AlmostKilled | width=67%]\n\nConsidering this was only page 10, this is an absolutely <b><i>astonishing</i></b> amount of material thrust onto readers, and I can't imagine <i>anyone</i> who wasn't immediately captivated, eager to move on.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>I </i>sure was!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | FalsePretenses | width=12%] And yet, even with all these events that preceded this, Stan and Steve <b>still weren't done</b>!&nbsp; Peter is so desperate for money that he sells photos to Jameson to make it look as if his conclusion was <i>right</i> -- and immediately feels ashamed of himself.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=This is <i>brilliant</i> plotting!]\n\nNot only has the story piled a boatload of problems onto Peter Parker's plate, Jameson's claim that Electro is really Spider-Man in disguise, and Peter's alarming need for cash drives the title character to do something no reader would have expected:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=He cheats Jameson!]&nbsp; The fact that there is a new villain is almost <i>secondary</i> to the gnawing question in every reader's mind:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Would I have done the same thing?\"]\n\nOnly when Electro himself reads the new headlines do we get the backstory about how an \"ordinary electric lineman named Max Dillon\" became Electro.&nbsp; It was nice to get his origin, particularly since it keeps Electro from feeling like a disposable obstacle dropped into the story, and page 12 focuses on the new villain-of-the-month moving onto the next phase of his dastardly plan to free inmates from a \"federal pen\" to serve as \"my flunkies!\"\n\n[ASSET: left | TouchingMoments | width=16%] But even <i>these</i> events only serve as the backdrop of Aunt May's operation on pages 13 and 14, and the quiet vigil that Peter shares with what appears to be his one friend in the world, namely Betty Brant.\n[BR]\n[BR] The sight of Aunt May being wheeled into the operating room and Peter sitting with Betty are [SPAN: severity=success; text=deeply touching moments] that really have no equal in any other Marvel magazine and truly <i>do</i> drive home the \"human interest\" in a way no other book even <i>approaches</i>.\n\n[ASSET: right | MoreForeshadowing | width=28%] While the prison riot plays in the background, Aunt May's operation is a success.&nbsp; The relief Peter feels seems to alert Betty, who now fears for his safety as he once again contemplates putting himself in danger for <i>Daily Bugle</i> photos.\n[BR]\n[BR] Only then do we learn that there is more to her worry than meets the eye when she expresses this fear aloud, saying that it's \"just like -- someone <i>else </i>I once knew--\" [SPAN: severity=success; text=Stan and Steve <i>do it again</i>!&nbsp; The human interest keeps piling on!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And by this time, there isn't a reader alive who isn't immediately flipping to the next page, desperate for <i>more</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut of course, the name of the magazine is, after all, [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=The Amazing Spider-Man], and now that Peter's worry about his aunt is over (she's going to be okay), he <i>finally</i> heads out to deal with Electro on page 15.\n\nAnd Steve's depiction of Jameson's face of surprise at seeing Spider-Man show up to deal with the new villain is <i>hilarious</i>, where he goes from jubilant, to shocked, to devastated -- all in the span of three panels.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=What pitch perfect artwork!]\n[ASSET: center | JamesonReaction | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: right | NotTheButler | width=15%] Over the course of the next five pages, Stan and Steve wrap things up with Electro (no surprise there), and we at least get to see Spider-Man's disappointment that he doesn't even know who the villain is when unmasked.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's a small plot point, but nonetheless nice, and Stan's dialogue of what <i>might</i> have happened in a movie (\"Good heavens!&nbsp; The <i>Butler</i>!\") [SPAN: severity=success; text=is the perfect tone of self-aware mockery!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | RobbingHim | width=14%] The <i>piece de resistance</i> comes in the last two pages which show Peter actually <i>giving</i> Jameson his action photos of Spider-Man battling Electro, which (to Jameson) are worth enough to actually <i>forgive</i> him for faking the others.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's a [SPAN: severity=error; text=mite too tidy and <i>absolutely</i> a stumble in the episode.]&nbsp; It's hard to believe Jameson would ever trust Peter again, but the scene survives because [SPAN: severity=success; text=Ditko sells Jameson's shameless greed so completely], even to the point of the narrative showing him thinking, \"I'm <i>robbing </i>him!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Perfectly drawn, and <i>absolutely</i> in character!)]\n\nThe last page shows the emotional damage Peter has done with respect to Betty, as she confesses his penchant for heading into danger reminds her of someone else (not to mention the fact that she never finished high school to take the job at the <i>Daily Bugle</i>).\n\nThe confused couple have a brief spat, but their emotions eventually settle down, and the two walk off together into the distance as the issue closes.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe splash page didn't lie:&nbsp; this one was absolutely [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"chock-full of human interest and drama!\"]\n\nThe whiplash the inner pages contained kept the reader engaged throughout the entire magazine in a way that is [SPAN: severity=success; text=seemingly unparalleled and encapsulates everything readers like about Spider-Man] in the course of a single issue.\n\nEven the villain of the story at times [SPAN: severity=info; text=felt incidental and overshadowed] by all the other things going on (yet still got his moments in the spotlight on several pages).&nbsp; But while the plot eventually featured his downfall, it felt, in many ways, like a [SPAN: severity=warning; text=subplot] of sorts, almost as if he wasn't the main draw.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And let's be honest:&nbsp; <i>was he?</i>)]\n\nVisually, Electro was portrayed nicely, and the odd bolts coming out of his hands are almost Doctor Strange-like, which is no surprise, given that both are depicted by the same artist.&nbsp; And we even got to see his origin, meaning he wasn't <i>completely</i> disregarded.\n\nBut I have to say that [SPAN: severity=info; text=what made the issue great was the human interest side of things], namely Peter's concern over his aunt's health, the shame he felt after cheating Jameson, and the mystery of this other person in Betty's past.\n\nJameson's constant tirade about Spider-Man was, of course, about what you'd expect, and his fiendish delight over having the last laugh over Peter in the closing moments is [SPAN: severity=success; text=not to be missed!]&nbsp; He plays almost like comic relief, at least in <i>this</i> story (and, I might remind you, in [LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-20] as well, perhaps a sign of things to come).\n\nPut it all together, and you get an issue that both satisfies and also leaves us with an eagerness to learn more.&nbsp; This is the first issue of Spider-Man where I felt like there just weren't enough pages -- I wanted <i>more</i>.\n\nIn fact, [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"I <i>need</i> the next issue -- <b>now!</b>\"]\n\nIt wasn't perfect, but it was by far the most complete adventure, filled with intrigue, villainy, human interest, drama, and the tantalizing <i>hint</i> of more to come...\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=In other words, <i>exactly</i> what the splash page promised!]",
  "letters_page": "Opinion seems split on whether Steve Ditko's art is any good, but most think his work on Spider-Man himself is adequately quirky.\n\nThe announcements page indicates that this issue was sent to the printers before [LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-20] even hit the stands, so they still don't know if they'll get the okay from the fans about dropping the letters page in <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Speaking for myself, I can't wait to see the avalanche of nay-sayers!]",
  "highlight": "Electro brings the electricity, but Spider-Man's personal life provides the sparks."
}